<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Weimar Constitution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Constitution of the German Reich, Die Verfassung des Deutschen Reichs, Weimarer Verfassung</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> Constitution, formally the Constitution of the German Reich, governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>. Drafted chiefly by Hugo Preuss and adopted by the National Assembly at Weimar on 31 July 1919 by 262 votes to 75, it created a federal semi-presidential republic with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> elected by universal suffrage and proportional representation, and guaranteed freedom of speech and of religion. Its Article 48 gave the president broad emergency powers; it was invoked 205 times before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> became chancellor and in 1933 was the basis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag-fire-decree">Reichstag Fire Decree</a>. Set aside in practice by the Enabling Act of 1933, the constitution was replaced in 1949.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/weimar-constitution.jpg" alt="Weimar Constitution" width="330" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JonRoma · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weimar_Constitution.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
